# Intro
This example reproduces the classic Boss & Bodenheimer (1979) collapse test: a
uniform-density, rigidly-rotating sphere of gas with an m=2 azimuthal density
perturbation. The perturbation grows as the sphere collapses, producing the
characteristic bar/two-arm fragmentation pattern, which makes this a standard
test for sink particle formation from gravitational fragmentation.

The default resolution contains 500k particles. `python3 makeIC.py --help` lists
every parameter (sphere radius, mass, rotation rate, perturbation amplitude, etc);
raise `-N` for production/paper-quality runs, and reduce
`Gravity:max_physical_baryon_softening` accordingly. `GEARSink:cut_off_radius`
and `GEARSink:density_threshold_Hpcm3` are coupled (the smoothing-length
formation criterion needs kernel_gamma*h < cut_off_radius, and h shrinks with
resolution). Rescale both together, not just one.

Sink particles are expected to start forming around 1.3-1.6 free-fall times
(t_ff ~= 3689 internal units for the default sphere).

# Configure
To run this example with the GEAR model,

    ./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-feedback=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-equation-of-state=isothermal-gas --with-grackle=path/to/grackle

and then

    make -j

# ICs
The run.sh script calls `makeIC.py` with default values. Run
`python3 makeIC.py --help` to see all the available options.

# Run
Type `./run.sh` (`N=<value>` overrides the particle number), and let's go!

Sink particles form and accrete gas in this test, but do not spawn stars
(`GEARSink:disable_star_formation: 1` in params.yml). This test targets
sink formation/accretion physics, not the stellar feedback loop.
